Even though it does not matter what the precise formulation is, we spell it out: the problem is to show that given an object $\mathcal{G}_0/\mathcal{K}_0$ of $\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})/\mathcal{K}_0$ and an isomorphism <div class="equation">
  \[  \alpha : \mathcal{G}_0 \times _{\mathcal{K}_0, \mathcal{K}(\delta ^1_1)} \mathcal{K}_1 \to \mathcal{G}_0 \times _{\mathcal{K}_0, \mathcal{K}(\delta ^1_0)} \mathcal{K}_1  \]
</div> over $\mathcal{K}_1$ satisfying a cocycle condition in $\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})/\mathcal{K}_2$, there exists $\mathcal{F}$ in $\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ and an isomorphism $\mathcal{F} \times \mathcal{K}_0 \to \mathcal{G}_0$ over $\mathcal{K}_0$ compatible with $\alpha $.